// Second path: Spanish, Italian and French through Telugu.
// Row format: [meaning, Telugu, Spanish, Italian, French]
// A language cell is "text" (Telugu reading generated by rule for Spanish/Italian) or ["text", "Telugu reading"].
// French cells always carry a hand-written reading, since French spelling is not read by rule.
// "—" means the language has no equivalent; a row is hidden when every shown language is "—".
//
// Prose (big, p, tip, h, cols) can depend on which languages are switched on, for both paths:
//   ⟦cond|text⟧  shows text only when every token in cond holds. Tokens: kn ta es it fr (that language
//                is on), !xx (it is off), one / many (exactly one / more than one language of this path is on).
//   ⟦#langs⟧     the names of the languages of this path that are on: "Spanish", "Spanish and French", …
//   ⟦#langs+te⟧  the same with Telugu in front: "Telugu, Spanish and French".
// A section can carry only: "cond" to appear only when cond holds; a row can end with { only: "cond" }.
